Original commit body from a6a9eb7226bfe753fd120ad94a400e7c0cc968ab:

WI-gapir. _compute_cyclomatic_complexity counted `with` / `async with`, and
its own docstring listed "with statements" among the decision points — the
deviation was stated rather than hidden, which is what made it decidable.

A context manager introduces no alternative path through the function; it is
setup/teardown, not a branch. Both the schema description and the spec commit
to "McCabe cyclomatic complexity (decision points + 1)", and the reference
implementations of that definition (flake8's mccabe, radon) do not count it.
So the code deviated from the standard it names.

CONSUMER-VISIBLE: this lowers Symbol.cyclomatic_complexity on any Python
function using a context manager (7.9% of them on the self-corpus) and
therefore shifts which clusters `repeat-finder --min-complexity` treats as
trivial. That is the correct consequence — a straight-line function whose
only "branch" was a `with` genuinely is a trivial cluster.

Python-only by construction: BRANCH_NODE_TYPES["python"] is empty, so Python
uses this AST path exclusively and no tree-sitter language shares the change.
Every other cyclomatic_complexity assertion in the tree belongs to a language
with its own computation (cmake, C, erlang, commonlisp, r, elm, nix).

Blast radius in tests was measured before changing anything and came to
exactly one assertion: test_cyclomatic_complexity_with_context_managers,
which pinned "with statements add 1 each" and so encoded the defect as
expected behaviour — the same shape as the WI-kalub guard that had to be
rewritten rather than resurrected when its clause was deleted. It is
inverted, and paired with a positive control asserting that real branches
INSIDE a `with` block still count, so a too-broad change that stopped
descending into the block would fail rather than pass.

2375 passed across the 58-file affected set.

